  <h2>Is Saily or AloSIM the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Saily if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You'd normally pay for a VPN or ad-blocker separately when traveling anyway</li>
        <li>Multi-country trip where you don't want a new eSIM profile at every border</li>
        <li>You're a frequent enough flyer that the $59.99/month Ultra tier's lounge passes could pay for themselves</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with AloSIM if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You want a free second phone number for verification texts or hotel bookings without handing out your real one</li>
        <li>You want the choice of a fixed-GB or unlimited plan from the same provider</li>
        <li>You want to buy ahead of a trip and only activate once you land, keeping AloSIM's refund window open</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Saily is currently blocked from being purchased or topped up while you're physically inside Turkey.</strong> It's one of several eSIM providers, alongside Airalo and Holafly, hit by a mid-2025 block from Turkey's telecom regulator (BTK). An eSIM bought and installed before you land still works once you're there; you just can't buy or top up in-country. We haven't seen AloSIM named in the same reporting, but if Turkey is on the itinerary, the safer move either way is to buy and install before you land.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>AloSIM's refund guarantee only covers eSIMs that never activate.</strong> If your eSIM won't connect or your phone turns out incompatible, AloSIM will refund it. Once you've activated a plan and started using data, that window closes even if you've barely touched your allowance. Saily's refund policy is similar in spirit (full refund if never installed, or if you've used under 1% of the data), so neither provider is generous once a plan is genuinely in use.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Saily vs AloSIM FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Saily or AloSIM</h3>
      <p>They're within a few cents of each other per GB before any code. Saily runs slightly cheaper in the destinations we checked; AloSIM's 15% code closes most of that gap.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which one includes a VPN or security tools</h3>
      <p>Saily. Every plan bundles ad blocking, malware/phishing site blocking, and a virtual-location tool, since Saily is built by NordVPN's parent company, Nord Security. AloSIM has no equivalent.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which one gives me a phone number</h3>
      <p>AloSIM, through a free partnership with Hushed, set up inside the AloSIM app after purchase. Saily doesn't offer an equivalent phone number feature.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I get a refund on either if I don't end up using the data</h3>
      <p>Only if you never activated the eSIM (or, on Saily, used under 1% of your data). Once a plan is genuinely in use on either provider, the refund window is closed.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

